            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Iron generally symbolizes strength, hardness, and unyielding power, frequently representing severe rule, the Messianic 'rod of iron,' or the crushing force of worldly empires (such as Daniel's fourth kingdom).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Iron specifically symbolizes the crushing strength of Daniel's fourth kingdom, identified historically as Rome.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Strength, hardness, and unyielding or crushing power.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The crushing strength of Daniel's fourth kingdom (Rome).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the symbol of iron to a single prophetic entity (Daniel's fourth kingdom / Rome), whereas the consensus views it as a broader symbol of strength and power applicable to various entities, including Christ's rule, Egyptian bondage, and general human stubbornness.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The consensus agrees that iron represents crushing strength and specifically applies it to Daniel's fourth kingdom (often identified as Rome), but the book elevates this specific application to the exclusive definition of the symbol.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's definition is a direct subset of the consensus. It takes one prominent scriptural application of iron—the crushing fourth empire of Daniel 2—and makes it the sole referent, narrowing the broader symbolic category of 'strength and unyielding power.'\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The biblical text uses iron symbolically in many contexts entirely unrelated to Rome or Daniel's fourth kingdom, such as the 'iron furnace' of Egypt (Deut 4:20) or the Messianic 'rod of iron' (Ps 2:9, Rev 2:27), meaning the book's definition is overly restrictive.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
